WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.031
Validation is where threat hunting becomes disciplined analysis.

00:00:03.031 --> 00:00:07.428
Not every unusual event is suspicious, not every suspicious event is malicious,

00:00:07.428 --> 00:00:09.993
and not every malicious-looking event is confirmed.

00:00:09.993 --> 00:00:13.657
These words should mean different things in notes and reports.

00:00:13.657 --> 00:00:17.321
Unusual means the activity differs from a baseline or expectation.

00:00:17.321 --> 00:00:21.718
Suspicious means the activity has characteristics that could align with adversary behavior.

00:00:21.718 --> 00:00:25.382
Malicious means there is evidence of harmful or unauthorized activity.

00:00:25.382 --> 00:00:30.511
Confirmed means the evidence has reached the threshold your organization uses for incident handling.

00:00:30.511 --> 00:00:32.710
A validation workflow starts with context.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.542
Compare the event against baselines.

00:00:34.542 --> 00:00:37.473
Has this user authenticated from this location before?

00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:40.038
Does this server normally run administrative scripts?

00:00:40.038 --> 00:00:42.969
Is the process common for that software package?

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:46.266
Is the destination domain part of a vendor service?

00:00:46.266 --> 00:00:50.663
Baselines are not perfect, but they help identify what requires deeper review.

00:00:50.663 --> 00:00:53.228
Next, check asset role and identity context.

00:00:53.228 --> 00:00:57.625
A command that is normal on an administrator jump host may be

00:00:57.625 --> 00:00:59.457
unusual on a finance workstation.

00:00:59.457 --> 00:01:04.953
A service account authenticating at night may be expected if it runs a scheduled job,

00:01:04.953 --> 00:01:08.617
but suspicious if it appears on endpoints it never touches.

00:01:08.617 --> 00:01:13.746
A developer workstation may generate traffic that would be odd for a point-of-sale system.

00:01:13.746 --> 00:01:16.678
Context prevents flat rules from becoming false positives.

00:01:16.678 --> 00:01:17.777
Review related events.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:22.906
A single process event may look odd, but the parent process, command line, user,

00:01:22.906 --> 00:01:24.372
network connection, file write,

00:01:24.372 --> 00:01:27.303
and surrounding authentication events can change the interpretation.

00:01:27.303 --> 00:01:31.700
If a DNS request is unusual, identify the clients, timing, proxy activity,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.898
and endpoint process context when available.

00:01:33.898 --> 00:01:38.295
If a privileged logon is unusual, review the source, target, authentication type,

00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:39.761
and nearby administrative actions.

00:01:39.761 --> 00:01:41.226
Look for business explanations.

00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:44.524
Maintenance windows, software deployment tools, vulnerability scanners, backup jobs,

00:01:44.524 --> 00:01:47.822
and help desk activity can all produce strange patterns.

00:01:47.822 --> 00:01:50.753
Validating benign explanations does not weaken the hunt.

00:01:50.753 --> 00:01:55.516
It improves the conclusion and may produce useful allow lists or tuning guidance.

00:01:55.516 --> 00:01:56.982
Preserve evidence before escalation.

00:01:56.982 --> 00:02:01.012
Save relevant event identifiers, query outputs, timestamps, affected assets, user context,

00:02:01.012 --> 00:02:03.577
screenshots if appropriate, and the queries used.

00:02:03.577 --> 00:02:07.241
Do not alter systems or collect data beyond your authorization.

00:02:07.241 --> 00:02:10.538
If evidence suggests active compromise, credential misuse, unauthorized access,

00:02:10.538 --> 00:02:14.569
or material risk, escalate to incident response through the defined process.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:15.668
Careful language matters.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:20.065
Say what the evidence shows, what it suggests, and what remains unknown.

00:02:20.065 --> 00:02:25.561
That discipline protects the credibility of the hunt and helps responders prioritize the right work.

00:02:25.561 --> 00:02:29.591
False positives are not failures when they teach the team something.

00:02:29.591 --> 00:02:34.354
A false positive can reveal a legitimate administrative workflow, a missing asset tag,

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:38.018
a noisy service account, or a detection that needs enrichment.

00:02:38.018 --> 00:02:39.118
Record that learning.

00:02:39.118 --> 00:02:44.980
The next hunt or alert rule can use the context to reduce noise without losing visibility.
